# .gitattributes
#
# ================================================================================================
# `* text=auto eol=lf` IS LOAD-BEARING HERE. IT IS NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE.
# ================================================================================================
# This repository is developed on WSL2 and Windows and runs entirely in Linux containers. Git's
# default on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) checks files out with CRLF endings — and every file that
# gets bind-mounted or COPY'd into a container then carries a stray \r that nothing strips.
#
# The failures are silent in exactly the places that matter, and each one names the wrong problem:
#
#   *.sh   `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M: no such file or directory` — an error naming a
#          file that plainly exists, on a line that looks correct in every editor.
#
#   php.ini  QUIETEST OF ALL. `output_buffering = 0\r` is not a valid integer, so PHP discards it
#          and falls back to the default of 4096. There is no warning. Every SSE answer is then
#          withheld until 4 KB accumulates, so a short answer arrives in one blob at the end and a
#          long one arrives in jerks — a bug that reproduces only in production, only through the
#          full edge, and looks like a slow model.
#
#   *.conf  nginx and php-fpm: `fastcgi_buffering off;\r` is a syntax error nginx reports with a
#          line number and no visible cause, and the container restart-loops.
#
#   valkey.conf  `maxmemory-policy noeviction\r` — Valkey refuses to start, which is the good
#          outcome. `save ""\r` on the cache instance is the bad one.
#
#   Dockerfile  a CRLF continuation line silently truncates the RUN that follows it.
#
# `eol=lf` forces LF in the WORKING TREE, not merely in the repository. That is the difference
# between this file working and not: `text=auto` alone normalizes on commit and would still check
# out CRLF on Windows.
#
# .editorconfig is the other half — it governs what an editor writes; this governs what git checks
# out. Both are needed.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ================================================================================================
# Explicit text, so a file with unusual bytes is never misdetected as binary
# ================================================================================================
*.php     text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.ts      text eol=lf
*.tsx     text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.jsx     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.conf    text eol=lf
*.ini     text eol=lf
*.acl     text eol=lf
*.env     text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf
Dockerfile        text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*      text eol=lf
.dockerignore     text eol=lf
Makefile          text eol=lf

# Windows-only files that must KEEP CRLF. Rare here, listed so nobody has to wonder.
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# ================================================================================================
# BINARY — never diffed, never normalized
# ================================================================================================
# samples/ is the golden evaluation corpus. Its fixtures are asserted BYTE FOR BYTE: content
# hashes, recrawl change detection, and extraction output all depend on the exact bytes. Any
# normalization here does not produce a formatting diff, it produces an evaluation regression that
# looks like a model or parser change.
samples/**/*.pdf   binary
samples/**/*.docx  binary
samples/**/*.xlsx  binary
samples/**/*.pptx  binary
samples/**/*.png   binary
samples/**/*.jpg   binary
samples/**/*.jpeg  binary
samples/**/*.tiff  binary
samples/**/*.webp  binary
samples/**/*.zip   binary

# The crawl fixture tree under samples/corpus/documents/site/www/ is HTML/XML/TXT and stays text —
# but its line endings must not move either, because the crawl fixture asserts on the served bytes
# and on nginx's ETag, which is derived from mtime AND CONTENT LENGTH. A CRLF checkout changes the
# length of every file and every recrawl reports "changed".
samples/corpus/documents/site/www/** text eol=lf

# Everything else binary
*.pdf     binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.webp    binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
*.eot     binary
*.zip     binary
*.gz      binary
*.tar     binary
*.br      binary
*.safetensors binary
*.onnx    binary
*.bin     binary
*.pt      binary
*.pth     binary

# ================================================================================================
# GENERATED — collapsed in review, excluded from language statistics
# ================================================================================================
# linguist-generated hides these from a PR diff by default and drops them from the repository's
# language breakdown. It does NOT make them un-reviewable: a lockfile change is still reviewed, by
# reading the manifest diff beside it, which is the reviewable artifact. What this prevents is a
# 40 000-line pnpm-lock.yaml burying the four lines that matter in the same PR.
composer.lock    linguist-generated=true -diff
pnpm-lock.yaml   linguist-generated=true -diff
uv.lock          linguist-generated=true -diff
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true -diff

packages/contracts/generated/**  linguist-generated=true
# NOTE: packages/design-tokens/generated/ is deliberately NOT marked generated and is NOT
# gitignored — the tokens are consumed by both apps and a visual change there must be visible in
# review.

# ================================================================================================
# EXPORT — kept out of `git archive` / release tarballs
# ================================================================================================
.github/         export-ignore
.claude/         export-ignore
docs/            export-ignore
samples/         export-ignore
scripts/security/rules/ export-ignore
